@begin(section)

@title(Get zick)

zick ships prebuilt binaries for Linux, macOS, and Windows.  They are
published on the
@link[uri="https://github.com/djha-skin/zick/releases"](GitHub Releases
page) for every tagged release.  Prefer a prebuilt binary; building
from source is only needed if you want the very latest commits or a
platform we do not build for.

@begin(section)
@title(Prebuilt binaries)

Each release page carries the following artifacts:

@begin(list)
@item(@b(Linux) — a generic @c(zick-linux-x86_64.tar.gz) tarball that
runs on any modern glibc distribution, plus native packages for the
distros we build on: @c(.deb) for Debian stable and Ubuntu 24.04/26.04,
and @c(.rpm) for Fedora 43/44.  Source packages (@c(.dsc), @c(.src.rpm))
ride along for distros we do not package, such as RHEL.)
@item(@b(macOS) — @c(zick-macos-arm64.tar.gz) for Apple Silicon.)
@item(@b(Windows) — @c(zick-windows-x64.zip), a @c(zick.exe) with the
SBCL runtime DLLs bundled beside it.)
@end(list)

@end(section)

@begin(section)
@title(Installing on Linux)

Download the generic tarball and put the executable on your
@c(PATH):

@begin[lang=sh](code)
curl -fSsL -o zick.tar.gz \\
  https://github.com/djha-skin/zick/releases/download/v0.1.0/zick-linux-x86_64.tar.gz
tar xzf zick.tar.gz
install -m755 zick ~/.local/bin/zick
@end(code)

Or install a native package for your distro:

@begin[lang=sh](code)
# Debian or Ubuntu
sudo dpkg -i zick_0.1.0_amd64.deb
# Fedora
sudo dnf install ./zick-0.1.0-1.x86_64.rpm
@end(code)

@end(section)

@begin(section)
@title(Installing on macOS)

Download the tarball, extract it, and move the binary onto your
@c(PATH):

@begin[lang=sh](code)
curl -fSsL -o zick.tar.gz \\
  https://github.com/djha-skin/zick/releases/download/v0.1.0/zick-macos-arm64.tar.gz
tar xzf zick.tar.gz
install -m755 zick /usr/local/bin/zick
@end(code)

(The build targets Apple Silicon only; Intel Macs must build from
source.)  The binaries are unsigned, so Gatekeeper will block the
first launch: right-click the binary in Finder and choose @b(Open), or
run @c(xattr -dr com.apple.quarantine /usr/local/bin/zick) once.

@end(section)

@begin(section)
@title(Installing on Windows)

Download @c(zick-windows-x64.zip), extract it, and add the folder to
your @c(PATH) (or move @c(zick.exe) somewhere already on it).  The
@c(libsbcl.dll) and MinGW runtime DLLs must stay in the same folder as
@c(zick.exe); they are bundled in the zip for that reason.

The executable is unsigned, so SmartScreen shows an @i(unknown
publisher) warning the first time: choose @b(More info) then @b(Run
anyway).

@end(section)

@begin(section)
@title(Building from Source)

zick is a Common Lisp program, built with
@link[uri="https://www.sbcl.org/"](SBCL) and
@link[uri="https://github.com/ocicl/ocicl"](ocicl).  To build it
yourself:

@begin[lang=sh](code)
git clone https://github.com/djha-skin/zick.git
cd zick
@end(code)

Install the dependencies (this uses the @c(ocicl.csv) manifest in the
repository root):

@begin[lang=sh](code)
ocicl install
@end(code)

Build the executable:

@begin[lang=sh](code)
./scripts/build
@end(code)

The build script bakes the large heap and control-stack sizes zick
needs for big repository resolutions into the binary, so no wrapper or
environment variables are required at run time.  The result is
@c(./com.djhaskin.zick) in the project root.  You can rename it to
@c(zick) and put it on your @c(PATH):

@begin[lang=sh](code)
ln -s "$PWD/com.djhaskin.zick" ~/.local/bin/zick
@end(code)

@end(section)

@begin(section)
@title(Checking the Installation)

Run zick with no arguments; it prints a short usage summary:

@begin[lang=sh](code)
zick
@end(code)

And @c(zick help) prints the full CLIFF help page: how options are
gathered, the alias table, and the list of subcommands.

@end(section)

@end(section)
